  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Signals and systems', note: 'A signal is any quantity varying with time (voltage, sound, temperature, pixel rows). A system transforms signals (amplifies, filters, delays). Signal processing is the discipline of that transformation.' },
    { math: '2 · Fourier: every signal is a chord', note: 'Any waveform decomposes into sine waves of different frequencies (Fourier, 1807). The equaliser on your music app IS Fourier\'s theorem with sliders — and the insight powers compression, filtering and analysis of everything periodic or not.' },
    { math: '3 · Sampling: analogue becomes digital', note: 'Nyquist–Shannon: sample at more than twice the highest frequency and the original is fully recoverable. Below that: aliasing (wagon wheels spinning backwards — frequencies masquerading as lower ones). CD audio at 44.1 kHz exists because hearing tops out ~20 kHz.' },
