<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/advanced/signal-processing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Signal processing is <strong>the mathematics of extracting meaning from variation</strong> —
    Fourier\'s insight that signals are sums of frequencies, Shannon\'s sampling law that makes
    digital possible, and the filtering craft that keeps the wanted and rejects the rest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="signals, Fourier, sampling, filtering" widget="equationStep" config={sigCfg} />

  <h2>Where it runs the world</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every digital device:</strong> audio, images, radio, Wi-Fi (OFDM = thousands of parallel Fourier channels), radar, medical imaging (the CT scan is Fourier mathematics through a body — the medicine lesson\'s imaging, computed), seismology (the earth lesson\'s wave reading).</li>
    <li><strong>Compression is signal processing:</strong> JPEG throws away frequencies the eye weighs less (discrete cosine transform); MP3 throws away sounds the ear masks. A billion photos a day exist affordably because of coefficient budgeting.</li>
    <li><strong>Noise is the enemy, statistics the ally:</strong> lock-in amplifiers pull signals from noise a million times larger; averaging trades time for certainty (the statistics ladder\'s SE shrinking as √n, in hardware form).</li>
    <li><strong>The digital revolution\'s law:</strong> Shannon\'s capacity theorem (bits per second ≤ bandwidth × log(1 + SNR)) sets the ceiling every generation of modems has climbed toward for 75 years — theory leading engineering by decades, exactly as Maxwell led wireless.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fourier (1807); Nyquist (1928) & Shannon (1948, 1949); Smith, The Scientist and Engineer\'s Guide to Digital Signal Processing (free).</p>
</TopicPage>
